## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical knowledge of securing software supply chains in cloud-native environments, addressing real-world security risks that SaaS platforms face when handling third-party plugins and dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) generation and analysis
- Dependency pinning with cryptographic hashes
- Plugin attestation and private registry management
- Pull request gates for drift detection and reproducible builds
- Incident response protocols for compromised plugins (72-hour SLA)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: Attestation verification for plugin registry
const verifyPluginAttestation = async (pluginId, attestation) => {
  const isValidSignature = await crypto.verify(attestation.signature, publicKey);
  if (!isValidSignature) throw new Error('Invalid plugin attestation');

  const sbom = await generateSBOM(pluginId);
  const hasVulnerabilities = await scanVulnerabilities(sbom);

  return { isValidSignature, hasVulnerabilities, sbom };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design defense-in-depth security controls in a Kubernetes multi-tenant fintech environment, focusing on namespace isolation, secure image supply chains, and incident response readiness.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes RBAC and namespace isolation
- Container image signing (cosign/Sigstore) and SBOMs
- Policy enforcement with OPA/Gatekeeper
- Service mesh mTLS and network policies
- CI/CD pipeline security checks for secrets
- Incident response with credential rotation and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: verify signed image (pseudo-code)
const cosign = require('@sigstore/cosign');

async function verifyImage(imageRef, publicKey) {
  try {
    const verification = await cosign.verify(imageRef, { publicKey });
    if (!verification.verified) {
      throw new Error('Image signature verification failed');
    }
    return verification;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Verification error:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

